{"kind": "report", "step": 19, "ts": "2026-11-19T15:15:31.321469+00:00", "data": {"category": "dns", "host": "dns1.lan", "component": "dns_rate_limit", "cause": "The rate limiter on dns1.lan's primary-dns (ratelimit: 20 q/s aggregated over the whole 10.20.0.0/24 client subnet) briefly exceeds its threshold a few times a day and silently drops whole bursts of LAN queries; when that happens the client's resolver falls through to its second server, gw.lan, whose unbound has private-address: 10.0.0.0/8 filtering that suppresses the forwarded 10.20.0.50 answer for nas.lan, so the client gets an empty NOERROR answer \u2014 getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND \u2014 until the burst passes and dns1 answers again a minute later.", "evidence": ["dns1 config sets ratelimit: 20 with ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24, i.e. one shared budget for all clients in 10.20.0.0/24 (cat /opt/primary-dns/config.yaml)", "dns1 journal shows exactly the sporadic episodes described: 'ratelimit: 10.20.0.0/24 exceeded 20 q/s, dropping 23/31/8 queries' at 02:14, 09:41 and 13:02, each 'back under limit' within seconds (journalctl -u primary-dns) \u2014 brief drops explain alerts that self-clear", "nas.lan resolves fine from dns1 when not rate-limited: ANSWER nas.lan A 10.20.0.50, TTL 10 (dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan on mon.lan)", "The same query to the fallback server gw.lan returns NOERROR with ANSWER: 0 \u2014 an empty answer, which glibc reports as ENOTFOUND (drill nas.lan @127.0.0.1 on gw.lan)", "gw.lan's unbound forwards lan. to 10.20.0.3 but also has private-address: 10.0.0.0/8 and only declares local-zone \"local.\" static, so the private answer for .lan is filtered out; internet names go through forward-first to DoT upstreams and are unaffected (cat /var/unbound/unbound.conf)", "mon.lan clients are configured with dns1 first, gw second (cat /etc/resolv.conf), so normal lookups succeed and only rate-limit bursts expose the empty gw answer"], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "Raise the rate limit on dns1.lan so it can't trip on aggregate LAN traffic \u2014 e.g. increase ratelimit well above the LAN's peak (hundreds of q/s) or set ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4 back to 32 so one client's burst doesn't drop everyone's queries. Additionally, fix gw.lan's unbound so its fallback answers aren't blanked: declare lan. as a passthrough/local zone instead of applying private-address filtering to forwarded .lan answers.", "detail": "primary-dns per-client /24 ratelimit (20 q/s) periodically drops LAN queries", "contributing": {"host": "gw.lan", "component": "dns_resolver_config"}}}
